  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Linear motion is the movement of an object along a straight path, such
    as a car travelling on a straight road. The object\u2019s speed may remain constant
    or change while its path stays straight.\n\nUniform linear motion occurs when
    an object covers equal distances in equal time intervals. Its speed remains constant.
    For example, a car travelling 10 metres every second along a straight road shows
    uniform linear motion.\n\nNon-uniform linear motion occurs when an object covers
    unequal distances in equal time intervals. Its speed changes, but its path remains
    straight. For example, a car speeding up travels farther during each successive
    second.\n\nAverage speed is calculated by dividing the total distance travelled
    by the total time taken:\n\nAverage Speed = Total Distance Travelled \xF7 Total
    Time Taken\n\nFor example, a car travelling 50 metres in 5 seconds has an average
    speed of 10 metres per second (m/s).\n\nEveryday examples of linear motion include
    a train moving along a straight track, an elevator moving up or down, and a drawer
    sliding in or out. In each case, the movement follows a straight line."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
